{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792367044,"nonce":"3eIW0B/d9IdYLCgGp35q+FiY2Tg=","message":"Corners and outline done as far as an offscreen render shows: the window is opened transparent (RAYDE_OPAQUE=1 fills it instead), the workbench's card inside the root takes a squircle radius for the macOS, GNOME and KDE styles when the window does not fill the screen, and a 1 px outline for Windows, GNOME and KDE. Pixels at the corner of an offscreen render over magenta: transparent at the corner for the rounded styles, the outline colour for Windows. The root itself cannot be clipped (the renderer treats it as the whole window), so the shape is on a card inside it. ashui confirmed hlwindow applies winit's default shadow for a borderless macOS window and that only a transparent window with a drawn shape has round corners.\n\nStill not seen on a real display (the capture here is black): whether the system shadow follows the rounded shape on macOS, how dragWindow and the edges feel, Windows and Linux, Wayland. The keys changed since the first comment: full screen is Ctrl+Cmd+F on macOS and Alt+Shift+Enter elsewhere, because F11 is Step Into.","files":null}]}